! ia_width_18bit.strap -- THE INTERRUPTION ADDRESS REGISTER IS 18 BITS:
! a store of ones into word 2 reads back as ones in bits 0-17 and zeros
! in bits 18-63.
!
! THE RULE, read from the page images (2026-11-19):
!   * Reference Manual printed p.48 (PDF p.51), "Interruption Address
!     Register": "The interruption address register contains 18 bits and
!     has address 2.0, which places it in the protected area of storage."
!   * printed p.35 (PDF p.38), "Interruption Address": "Bits 0-17 of
!     location 2 form the interruption address register."
!   * pocket card X22-6533-1 (docs/pcard-1.png), SPECIAL STORAGE
!     ASSIGNMENT: "2,P  Interruption Address  18  0-17".
!   * printed p.34 (PDF p.37), "Special Registers": "Not all bit
!     positions in locations 0-15 are assigned to specific functions.
!     Those bit positions that are not so assigned are always zero.
!     Information stored in these positions is not recoverable.
!     (However, for an exception see the programming note in the next
!     section.)"  That programming note (p.34) is location 1's bits
!     19-27 only, between the interval timer and the time clock.  Word 2
!     has no such note, so bits 18-63 of word 2 always read zero.
!
! ENTRY STATE.  The model resets with the interruption system disabled,
! and printed p.31 (PDF p.35) suspends protection while disabled, so the
! stores into the permanently protected word 2 take (the same reason
! jb_ax5b routine 1260 TEST 2, decks/ax5b_160_test2_ia.strap, can store
! there).  The deck still starts with BD,$+.32 so it says so itself.
!
! THREE STORE PATHS, each read back by an instruction into ordinary
! storage, not only by the testbench's look at word 2:
!   1. ST(BU,64,8),$IA of a 64-bit field of ones -- every bit of word 2,
!      so bit 18 is covered (a mask one bit too wide fails here; one too
!      narrow fails TEST 2).  Read back at once (RB1) and again after two
!      NOPs (RB2).
!   2. SV,$X1,$IA with a value of all ones -- STORE VALUE writes bits
!      0-24 (printed p.24), so ones land in 18-24: the case TEST 2's
!      header names as the evidence it could not see (RB3).
!   3. SX,$X2,$IA of an index word of all ones -- STORE INDEX, a full
!      64-bit word (RB4).
! Expected of each: 0xffffc00000000000 (IBM bits 0-17 ones).
!
! RESULT, 2026-11-19: PASS on the fixed model (StretchStorage.bsv's
! addrInterruptAddr arm keeps bits 0-17 only): RB1-RB4 and word 2 all
! 0xffffc00000000000.
!
! PROVEN DISCRIMINATING, OBSERVED (a scratch copy of the tree; the
! tracked bsv/ not touched):
!   INJECTION = THE UNFIXED MODEL (StretchStorage.bsv as at 7590ec8,
!     "addrInterruptAddr: interruptionAddress <= v;") -> FAIL on 5 pins:
!     RB1 0xffffffffffffffff, RB2 0xffffffffffffffff, RB3
!     0xffffff8000000000 (bits 0-24, STORE VALUE's field), RB4
!     0xffffffffffffffff, IA:2 0xffffffffffffffff (the last store, SX).
!     PC 4253 (PASS) passes: every store takes, only the width differs.
!   CONTROL: the fixed StretchStorage.bsv (the tracked tree, same deck and
!     pins): PASS, all five pins 0xffffc00000000000, PC 4253.
!
        SLC, (8)4100.0
START   BD,  $+.32                   ! disabled: word 2 is protected
        L(BU,64,8), ONES             ! accumulator := 64 ones
        ST(BU,64,8), $IA             ! 1. all of word 2
        L(BU,64,8), $IA              !    read back at once
        ST(BU,64,8), RB1
        NOP
        NOP
        L(BU,64,8), $IA              !    and again, later
        ST(BU,64,8), RB2
        Z,   $IA
        LV,  $X1, ONES               ! value field all ones
        SV,  $X1, $IA                ! 2. STORE VALUE: bits 0-24
        L(BU,64,8), $IA
        ST(BU,64,8), RB3
        Z,   $IA
        LX,  $X2, ONES               ! index word all ones
        SX,  $X2, $IA                ! 3. STORE INDEX: all 64 bits
        L(BU,64,8), $IA
        ST(BU,64,8), RB4
PASS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)6000.0
ONES    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
RB1     DD(BU,64), 0
RB2     DD(BU,64), 0
RB3     DD(BU,64), 0
RB4     DD(BU,64), 0
        END, START
